/**

\page CommandLineAppHowToPage How to create a command-line app

\tableofcontents

This page shows how to write a command-line app for MITK, how to declare
its arguments with mitkCommandLineParser, and how to register the
executable with CMake. For the apps that ship with MITK and their usage
see \ref MITKCmdAppsPage.

\section CommandLineAppHowToPageDescription What is a command-line app?

A command-line app is a small executable that performs one processing task
without a GUI, for example converting a file or fitting a model. It is
built on top of the MITK modules, so it uses the same readers, writers and
algorithms as the MITK Workbench. All apps share the same argument syntax
and the same self-description mechanism, provided by mitkCommandLineParser.

\section CommandLineAppHowToPageSetUp Setting up a command-line app

This section walks through <tt>ExampleToUpperCase.cpp</tt> from the example
module (<tt>Examples/FirstSteps/NewModule/cmdapps/</tt>). See
\ref NewModulePage for the suggested structure of a module and its
<tt>cmdapps/</tt> directory.

Each app has its own main function.

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp main

The first thing to do is to describe the app and configure the accepted
arguments with mitkCommandLineParser. Title, category, description and
contributor appear in the help text and in the XML self-description.

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp create parser

Our example accepts an input file name, an output file name and an optional
flag for verbose output.

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp add arguments

The following argument types are available for the addArgument method:
\li String
\li Bool
\li StringList
\li Int
\li Float
\li Directory
\li File
\li Image

The optional channel parameter (Input or Output) marks files and images as
input or output in the XML self-description. The label string is a short
human-readable name of the argument, the help string explains it.

Every argument is optional by default. Pass <tt>false</tt> for the optional
parameter to make it required. If a required argument is missing, the parser
prints the help text and returns an empty map, which is the conventional
signal for the app to exit with a failure code.

After the arguments have been declared, parseArguments is called.

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp parse the arguments

After all arguments have been parsed the actual processing can start. In this
case we read the file, convert the contained text to upper case and write the
new data to the specified output location.

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp do processing

\subsection CommandLineAppHowToPageSetUpExampleHelpOutput Example help output

Running the example without any argument or with wrong ones:
\code
./MitkExampleToUpperCase
\endcode

... will emit the following help text:

\verbatim
Command Line Utility *To Upper Case* (v2026.06) in Category *MITK-Examples*
An example command-line app that converts the contents of a text file to upper case.
German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)

Use --help to print this text.
Use --xml to generate an XML description parsable as a CTK Command Line Module Plugin.
Use --version to print MITK revision information.

Optional parameters
  -v, --verbose, Whether to produce verbose output (optional)

Required I/O parameters
  -i, --input, input file (.txt/.example)
  -o, --output, where to save the output (.txt/.example)
\endverbatim

The parser handles three informational arguments itself and terminates the
process with exit code 0 after printing: <tt>--help</tt> (or <tt>-h</tt>)
prints the help text, <tt>--version</tt> prints the Git commit hash and branch
name of the MITK build, and <tt>--xml</tt> prints the XML self-description
explained below. The short name <tt>-h</tt> is therefore reserved. Declaring a
help argument, as the example does, only makes it appear in the argument list
of the help text.

\section CommandLineAppHowToPageCMake Registering the executable with CMake

A command-line app is created with the CMake function
<tt>mitkFunctionCreateCommandLineApp</tt>, which wraps
<tt>mitk_create_executable</tt> and adds the MitkCommandLine module
dependency. The executable is named after the NAME argument with the module
prefix, so <tt>NAME ExampleToUpperCase</tt> produces
<tt>MitkExampleToUpperCase</tt>. By default <tt>NAME.cpp</tt> is compiled;
pass <tt>CPP_FILES</tt> to use other sources.

\code
option(BUILD_NewModuleCmdApps "Build command-line apps of the example module" OFF)

if(BUILD_NewModuleCmdApps)
  mitkFunctionCreateCommandLineApp(
    NAME ExampleToUpperCase
    DEPENDS MitkNewModule
  )
endif()
\endcode

Apps in MITK are grouped per module behind a <tt>BUILD_&lt;Module&gt;CmdApps</tt>
option that additionally honours <tt>MITK_BUILD_ALL_APPS</tt>. Installed apps
land in the <tt>bin/</tt> directory of a package together with a launcher
script in <tt>apps/</tt>, see \ref MITKCmdAppsPage. Pass
<tt>NO_INSTALL</tt> to exclude an app from packaging, as the example module
does.

Every app shipped with MITK also has a user manual page at
<tt>Modules/&lt;Module&gt;/documentation/Mitk&lt;App&gt;.md</tt> that is
linked as a subpage from \ref MITKCmdAppsPage.

\section CommandLineAppHowToPageXMLRepresentation Retrieving the XML argument description

If the app is executed with the argument <tt>--xml</tt>, the parser emits an
XML description of all declared arguments on the console and returns an empty
map, so the app exits without processing. The format follows the
<a href="https://www.slicer.org/wiki/Documentation/Nightly/Developers/SlicerExecutionModel">Slicer execution model</a>,
which external tools can use to generate a user interface or to integrate the
app into a pipeline. Title, category, description and contributor are taken
from the parser configuration:

\snippet ExampleToUpperCase.cpp create parser

All arguments are listed in one <tt>parameters</tt> element with the default
label "Parameters"; the groups of beginGroup / endGroup only affect the help
text. Label and description of the element can be set with the parser's
changeParameterGroup method, which must be called before adding the arguments.
An argument with the short name <tt>h</tt> is omitted from the XML since a
help flag has no use in a generated interface.

The XML is the only output in this case; the banner line "Running Command Line
Utility ..." that precedes a normal run is not printed.

\subsection CommandLineAppHowToPageXMLRepresentationExampleXMLOutput Example XML output

Running the example with argument <tt>--xml</tt> ...
\code
./MitkExampleToUpperCase --xml
\endcode

... will emit the following XML description:

\verbatim
<executable>
<category>MITK-Examples</category>
<title>To Upper Case</title>
<description>An example command-line app that converts the contents of a text file to upper case.</description>
<contributor>German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)</contributor>
<parameters>
<label>Parameters</label>
<description>Parameters</description>
<file>
<name>input</name>
<description>input file (.txt/.example)</description>
<label>Input file</label>
<longflag>input</longflag>
<flag>i</flag>
<channel>input</channel>
</file>
<file>
<name>output</name>
<description>where to save the output (.txt/.example)</description>
<label>Output file</label>
<longflag>output</longflag>
<flag>o</flag>
<channel>output</channel>
</file>
<boolean>
<name>verbose</name>
<description>Whether to produce verbose output</description>
<label>Verbose Output</label>
<longflag>verbose</longflag>
<flag>v</flag>
</boolean>
</parameters>
</executable>
\endverbatim

*/
